Subject: Elide The `var a = arguments` Alias

What this does
  Teaches the arguments-elision scan a fourth site kind: `var a = arguments;`
  The site emits nothing and swallows the adjacent put_loc, and every later read
  of the alias local is rewritten to read the argument frame directly, exactly
  as a direct read of `arguments` is.

Why we need it
  The scan elides the mapped `arguments` object only when every use is a
  recognised consumer read straight through get_loc. Copying it to a local first
  -- `var a = arguments; ... a[i] ... a.length` -- is not a recognised consumer,
  and one unrecognised use refuses the whole function, so the single most common
  shape in real code reified the object on every call.

What it adds
  No new opcode, no format change, no blob regeneration. The transform depends on
  proving that the store dominates every read: positional order is not enough,
  because `function f(x){ if (x) {} else { var a = arguments; } return a.length; }`
  stores before the read in position but not in execution, where `a` is undefined
  and reading it must throw. The store is therefore required to sit in the
  function's entry basic block -- no label, label-carrying branch,
  exception-region opener or block terminator may appear before it -- and the
  scan separately requires the alias to be written exactly once, never captured,
  and never re-aliased.

Needs a bytecode version bump
  No.

